finding 4: the ring contract's sentence
   What an element of a unital ring is: its quotient by the ring's own law. Division in a ring is multiplication by the inverse of the divisor, \(a / b = a b^{-1}\), so the quotient is taken on the right. For a ring that does not commute this is not \(b^{-1} a\), which is the scalar action of the same element on the ring regarded as a left module over itself. The operator is the coercion model's ``Element.__truediv__``: it brings both operands into one parent and asks ``_div_`` there. That parent is the one the operands meet in, and the quotient need not stay in it: \(\mathbf{Z}(3) / 2\) divides in \(\mathbf{Z}\), where \(3\) and the numeral \(2\) meet, and the answer \(3/2\) is an element of \(\mathbf{Q}\) because the realization's ``_div_`` says where the quotient lands. The realization that computes the division therefore owns the codomain: an engine-backed ring divides in its engine and reads the quotient in the parent the engine put it in; an exact field decides its zero and undecided-nonzero denominators before dividing; and a realization with neither keeps \(a b^{-1}\) from the monoid level (``sage.categories.monoids.Monoids.ElementMethods._div_``). What a ring element never an
